      "work": {
        "model_authored_report": {
          "summary": "Observation. The permitted window for the reviewed single name returns a short but contiguous run of daily unadjusted closes with no interior gaps other than an ordinary weekend break, exercising the full permitted span. The measured series is mildly two-sided: the daily-return statistics show both rising and falling sessions with falling sessions slightly outnumbering rising ones, and an average daily return that is negligible relative to the dispersion of daily moves. Hypothesis. Because the daily-return dispersion is large relative to the drift across the window, the simplest falsifiable price-only idea is a contrarian close-to-close rule: take the opposite sign of a short-horizon, volatility-scaled deviation of the close from its own short moving average, and carry that position to the next trading day's close. This is post-observation exploration of one fixed historical sample; it is not a backtest, not preregistered, and asserts no alpha and no gate outcome.",
          "proposed_formula": "r_t = close_t / close_{t-1} - 1; d_t = close_t / SMA_m(close)_t - 1; s_t = d_t / (k * sd_m(r)); position_t = -sign(s_t) if |s_t| >= z_min else 0; signal_time = close_t; execution = next_trading_day_close; parameters: m = 3, k = 1, z_min = 1.0",
          "recommended_changes": [
            "Widen the sample window substantially and include many names before drawing any inference, since a single name over a short span cannot separate name-specific noise from generic behaviour.",
            "Use adjusted closes in any follow-up so that corporate actions cannot masquerade as reversal or continuation.",
            "Pre-specify the lookback length, scaling constant and threshold before future data access, rather than selecting them after inspecting the window.",
            "Reserve a disjoint evaluation window so that the fit and the check are never the same observations.",
            "Report the count of aligned label cases next to every statistic, so that discarded tail signals are never silently folded into a result.",
            "State execution costs, slippage and close-to-close fill assumptions explicitly, since next-close execution is a modelling convention rather than a tradable promise."
          ],
          "limitations": [
            "Single name, no cross-sectional breadth: any observed pattern may be idiosyncratic to the reviewed name rather than a general daily-return regularity.",
            "Very short window with few usable return observations, so every statistic is dominated by sampling error and the estimate of dispersion is itself fragile.",
            "Closes are unadjusted, so any distribution or split inside or around the window would contaminate successive returns, although the scope declares unadjusted history.",
            "The bars are a current download of historical data rather than a vintage archive, so point-in-time availability of these closes cannot be guaranteed.",
            "Exchange-calendar completeness is unverified by the calculator: the alignment lags count returned observations, not confirmed trading sessions.",
            "Next-trading-day-close execution ignores intraday fills, price limits, suspension days, transaction costs and market impact.",
            "The idea was formed after seeing this window, so lookback and threshold choices are post-observation and carry an unquantified selection bias; nothing here is preregistered.",
            "Price-only inputs cannot condition on volatility regime, liquidity or news, so the rule has no mechanism to distinguish a genuine reversal from a liquidity or event-driven move."
          ],
